WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.477
A merge can run without an error and still produce the wrong analytical result.

00:00:05.477 --> 00:00:12.547
That is why it is useful to validate the relationship between keys and inspect which rows matched.

00:00:12.547 --> 00:00:17.382
In the example, orders are joined with customer information.

00:00:17.382 --> 00:00:22.867
The validate argument tells Pandas that many orders may belong to one customer.

00:00:22.867 --> 00:00:30.957
The indicator argument creates a column that tells us whether each row matched both tables or came from only one side.

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:34.887
This kind of checking is important in real projects.

00:00:34.887 --> 00:00:39.697
A duplicated customer key could unexpectedly multiply rows.

00:00:39.697 --> 00:00:43.792
A missing key could cause information to disappear.

00:00:43.792 --> 00:00:50.202
Both problems may go unnoticed if we only look at whether the code ran successfully.

00:00:50.202 --> 00:00:54.062
Run the merge and inspect the indicator column.

00:00:54.062 --> 00:01:00.382
Notice what happens to the order whose customer ID does not exist in the customer table.

00:01:00.382 --> 00:01:04.482
A reliable analysis does not stop at merging.

00:01:04.482 --> 00:01:06.127
It checks the merge.
